  "success": true,
  "output": "## Performance Analysis — Platizio (Next.js 16 / React 19 marketing site)\n\nContext that shapes every finding below: this is a **fully static, client-content-only** site — no database, no ORM, no API routes, and zero `fetch()` calls anywhere in `src/` (verified by grep). Every page uses `generateStaticParams`/`dynamicParams = false` and pre-renders at build time (e.g. `src/app/insights/[slug]/page.tsx:6-10`). That reframes two of your five categories, so I'll say so plainly rather than manufacturing examples that don't exist here.\n\n### 1. N+1 query patterns — **not applicable**\nNo database/ORM/fetch layer exists. All content (articles, products, team bios, legal text) is imported as static TS modules (`src/lib/articles.ts`, `src/lib/products.tsx`, `src/content/**`) and baked into HTML at build time. There is no query loop to have an N+1 problem in the first place.\n\n### 2. Unnecessary React re-renders — the real finding is at the RSC boundary, not inside components\n\nEvery full-page component is marked `\"use client\"` even though most of its content is static prose:\n- `src/components/pages/LegalPage.tsx:1`, `ArticlePage.tsx:1`, `InsightsIndex.tsx:1`, `ProductsIndex.tsx:1`, `AboutPage.tsx:1`, `ContactPage.tsx:1`\n\nThe reason each is client-side is a single small thing — `useReducedMotion()`, a `useState` filter, a hover flag — but Next's rule is that everything a Client Component **imports** (not just what it renders) is pulled into the client bundle, even if that imported module has no `\"use client\"` of its own. So `RichText.tsx` (`src/components/site/RichText.tsx:1-8`, explicitly commented \"Pure render — no client hooks — so it is cheap for long documents\") never actually gets to run as a Server Component: because it's imported into `LegalPage`/`ArticlePage`, it's compiled into the client bundle and hydrated anyway. That's most costly on `privacy-policy`/`terms-and-condition` (multi-thousand-word legal text) and the long-form articles — real content, fully static, shipped and hydrated as client JS for no interactive benefit.\n\n**Fix**: split the interactive sliver out and let the static body render server-side:\n```tsx\n// src/app/privacy-policy/page.tsx (Server Component, no \"use client\")\nexport default function Page() {\n  const doc = getLegalDoc(\"privacy-policy\")!;\n  return (\n    <LegalShell doc={doc}>       {/* client: just the FadeUp/motion wrapper */}\n      <RichText blocks={doc.blocks} />   {/* stays a server-rendered child */}\n    </LegalShell>\n  );\n}\n```\n`RichText` passed as `children` from a Server Component parent is never bundled client-side, even though `LegalShell` itself is `\"use client\"`.\n\nEverything else here is actually well-behaved:\n- Static arrays (`ARTICLES`, `PRODUCTS`, `QUOTES`, `TEAM`, `PRINCIPLES`, `MOTION_TAGS` in `Reveal.tsx:9`) are hoisted to module scope, not recreated per render.\n- `Nav.tsx:24` fires `setScrolled` on every scroll event, but since it sets the same boolean via `window.scrollY > 48`, React bails the re-render via `Object.is` whenever the value doesn't actually flip — not a real cost.\n- Framer Motion values (`useMotionValue`/`useSpring` in `MagneticButton.tsx`, `GrowthStat` in `Hero.tsx:59-60`) intentionally bypass React state, so high-frequency pointermove/scroll updates never trigger re-renders.\n\n### 3. Caching opportunities\n\n- `MarketConstellation` is already correctly code-split via `next/dynamic(..., { ssr: false })` in `Hero.tsx:16-19` — good, keep this pattern for any future heavy/optional client-only widget.\n- Pages are already statically generated (SSG) and get Next's default full-route cache — no manual caching layer is missing.\n- The one real opportunity is the RSC-boundary issue in §2: that's effectively a \"caching\" win too, since server-rendered static markup can be served from Next's route cache without any client JS/hydration cost, whereas the current all-client-component pages re-execute React on every navigation.\n\n### 4. Memory leaks — none found (verified, not assumed)\nChecked every `addEventListener`, `setInterval`/`requestAnimationFrame`, and object lifecycle in the codebase:\n- `Nav.tsx:26-27` scroll listener → removed in cleanup.\n- `MarketConstellation.tsx:140-141` pointermove listener → removed in cleanup.\n- `MarketConstellation.tsx:164-168` `matchMedia` change listener → removed in cleanup.\n- `SmoothScroll.tsx:16-21` rAF loop and `Lenis` instance → `cancelAnimationFrame` + `lenis.destroy()` on unmount.\n- `Preloader.tsx:56-60` rAF loop → cancelled on unmount.\n- `MarketConstellation.tsx:58` `geometry.dispose()` on unmount — correct WebGL hygiene.\n\nNo dangling subscriptions, timers, or undisposed GPU resources.\n\n### 5. Redundant computations — one real hotspot, one minor one\n\n**Real: `Terrain` in `MarketConstellation.tsx:60-69`.** Every animation frame (60fps, unconditionally whenever the section is mounted and not `prefers-reduced-motion`), `waveY()` — 3 sin/cos pairs — is recomputed for all `COLS * ROWS = 6,050` vertices on the **main JS thread**: ~36K trig calls/frame, ~2M+/sec, purely to animate a decorative hero background. It also keeps running via `frameloop=\"always\"` even if the user scrolls the Hero out of view — there's no visibility gate, only the existing reduced-motion gate.\n\n```tsx\n// src/components/three/MarketConstellation.tsx:60-69 — current: CPU, every vertex, every frame\nuseFrame((state) => {\n  if (reduced) return;\n  const t = state.clock.getElapsedTime();\n  const attr = geometry.getAttribute(\"position\") as THREE.BufferAttribute;\n  const arr = attr.array as Float32Array;\n  for (let i = 0; i < baseX.length; i++) {\n    arr[i * 3 + 1] = waveY(baseX[i], baseZ[i], t);\n  }\n  attr.needsUpdate = true;\n});\n```\n**Suggested fix**: move the displacement into a vertex shader (custom `ShaderMaterial` with a `uTime` uniform updated once per frame) so the GPU computes all 6,050 vertices in parallel instead of a JS `for` loop; additionally gate `frameloop` with an `IntersectionObserver` the same way `reduced` already gates it, so the scene pauses when scrolled off-screen.\n\n**Minor: `InsightsIndex.tsx:70-73`** recomputes `ARTICLES_SORTED.filter(...)` on every render, and `ArticlePage.tsx:24,26,28-30` recompute `buildToc`/`readingMinutes`/`related` the same way. With today's 9 articles this is free; it's only worth a `useMemo` if the article catalog grows substantially — flagging it now so it doesn't get missed later, not urging a change today.\n\n**Bottom line**: the codebase is clean on the categories that usually hide real bugs (leaks, redundant loops) — the one concrete, worthwhile change is un-clienting the static content pages so long-form text (legal docs, articles) stops shipping as hydrated client JS, plus optionally moving the hero's terrain animation onto the GPU.\n",
